NAME
       podman - manage containers and container images without a daemon

SYNOPSIS
       podman COMMAND [OPTIONS] [ARGUMENTS]

DESCRIPTION
       Podman runs containers: programs packed with everything they need (an
       image) and started in their own isolated environment. It needs no
       background service; each podman command does its work and exits, and
       a container is a normal process tree that systemd can manage.

       Podman comes with the container-tools package (dnf install
       container-tools), which also brings skopeo and buildah.

       Rootless and rootful. Any user can run podman. Each user has their own
       images and containers, stored in ~/.local/share/containers/storage;
       root's are in /var/lib/containers/storage, and neither sees the
       other's. Rootless containers cannot bind ports below 1024 and their
       root user is mapped to the real user. Run podman as the user who
       should own the containers: log in as that user (ssh user@localhost)
       rather than using su or sudo, so the user's session and
       XDG_RUNTIME_DIR are set up.

       Images are named REGISTRY/PATH:TAG, such as
       registry.lab.example.com/ubi9/ubi:latest. Without a tag, latest is
       used. A short name without a registry is looked up in the
       unqualified-search-registries of /etc/containers/registries.conf (or
       ~/.config/containers/registries.conf).

COMMANDS
       login [-u USER] [-p PASSWORD] [--password-stdin] REGISTRY
              Log in to a registry; the credentials are saved in
              $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/containers/auth.json. --get-login shows who is
              logged in.

       logout REGISTRY
              Forget the saved credentials.

       search [--list-tags] TERM
              Search registries for images.

       pull IMAGE
              Download an image.

       images [-q]
              List local images (podman image ls).

       rmi [-f] IMAGE...
              Remove images (-f also removes containers using them).

       tag IMAGE NEWNAME
              Give an image another name.

       build -t NAME [-f FILE] DIRECTORY
              Build an image from a Containerfile; see podman-build(1).

       run [OPTIONS] IMAGE [COMMAND]
              Create and start a container; see podman-run(1).

       create [OPTIONS] IMAGE [COMMAND]
              Create a container without starting it.

       ps [-a] [-q] [--format TEMPLATE]
              List running containers (-a: also stopped ones).

       start, stop [-t SECONDS], restart, kill CONTAINER...
              Start, stop (TERM, then KILL after the timeout), restart or
              kill containers. -a acts on all of them.

       rm [-f] CONTAINER...
              Remove containers (-f also stops a running one).

       logs [-f] CONTAINER
              Show what the container printed (-f: keep following).

       exec [-it] CONTAINER COMMAND
              Run a command inside a running container, such as
              podman exec -it web /bin/bash.

       inspect [-f TEMPLATE] NAME
              Show the details of a container or image as JSON, or only the
              fields a Go template picks, such as -f '{{.Config.User}}'.

       port CONTAINER
              Show the published ports.

       cp SOURCE TARGET
              Copy files between the host and a container (CONTAINER:PATH).

       volume create|ls|inspect|rm NAME
              Manage named volumes.

       generate systemd [OPTIONS] CONTAINER
              Write a systemd unit for a container (deprecated in favour of
              Quadlet); see podman-generate-systemd(1).

       unshare COMMAND
              Run a command inside the user namespace of rootless podman,
              for example to chown a directory to a UID inside the container.

       info   Show the configuration: storage, registries, rootless or not.

       system prune
              Remove stopped containers and unused data.

EXAMPLES
       Log in, find and download an image:

           podman login -u student registry.lab.example.com
           podman search registry.lab.example.com/ubi9
           podman pull registry.lab.example.com/ubi9/ubi:latest
           podman images

       Run a web server in the background on port 8080 and test it:

           podman run -d --name web -p 8080:8080 registry.lab.example.com/ubi9/httpd-24
           podman ps
           curl http://localhost:8080/

       Look inside, read the logs, clean up:

           podman exec -it web /bin/bash
           podman logs web
           podman stop web
           podman rm web

       Read one value from an image's settings:

           podman inspect -f '{{.Config.User}}' registry.lab.example.com/ubi9/httpd-24

       Inspect an image in the registry without downloading it:

           skopeo inspect docker://registry.lab.example.com/ubi9/ubi

FILES
       /etc/containers/registries.conf
              Registries for short names, insecure registries.

       ~/.local/share/containers/storage
              A rootless user's images, containers and volumes.

       ~/.config/containers/systemd/
              A rootless user's Quadlet files.

SEE ALSO
       podman-run(1), podman-build(1), podman-generate-systemd(1),
       podman-systemd.unit(5), skopeo(1), buildah(1), loginctl(1)
